      "text": "There are a total of $10 \\cdot 9 = 90$ ways to choose the first and the second member.\nHowever, since the members are chosen randomly, there is no additional constraint to consider.\nThus, the probability that the two chosen members are both girls is $\\dbinom{5}{2} = 10$ out of the $90$ total outcomes, which gives us a probability of $\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{9}}$.\n\nLet's break down the problem and the given solution step by step:\n\nGiven:\n- Total number of club members: 10\n- Boys: 5\n- Girls: 5\n\n**Required:**\n- Calculate the probability of choosing 2 girls from these members.\n\n### Step-by-Step Breakdown\n\n1. **Total number of ways to choose 2 members from 10**:\n   To determine the total number of ways to choose 2 members from 10, you would use the combination formula, which is given by:\n   \n   [ text{{NumMembers(total)}} choose text{{NumMembers()}} = frac{text{{NumMembers(total)}}!}{(text{{NumMembers(girls)}}! times text{{NumMembers(boys)}}!)} ]\n\n   The formula for combinations is:\n\n   [\n   text{{Combinations}} = frac{text{{NumMembers(total)}}!}{(text{{NumMembers(girls)}}! times text{{NumMembers(boys)}}!)}\n   ]\n\n   - Substitute [text{{NumMembers(total)}} = 10]\n   - Substitute [text{{NumMembers(girls)}} = 5]\n   - Substitute [text{{NumMembers(boys)}} = 5]\n\n2. **Total ways to choose 2 girls from 5**:\n   Similarly, the number of ways to choose 2 girls from the g which is [text{{NumMembers(girls)}}] is calculated as:\n\n   [ text{{NumMembers(bangles)}} choose text{{NumMembers(girls)}} = frac{text{{NumMembers(girls)}}!}{(text{{NumMembers(girls)}}! times (text{{NumMembers(bangles)}} - text{{NumMembers(girls)}})!)} ]\n\n   The formula for combinations is:\n\n   [\n   text{{Combinations}} = frac{text{{NumMembers(girls)}}!}{(text{{NumMembers(girls)}}! times (text{{NumMembers(bangles)}} - text{{NumMembers(girls)}})!)}\n   ]\n\n   - Substitute [text{{NumMembers(bangles)}} = 5]\n   - Substitute [text{{NumMembers(girls)}} = 5]\n\n3. **Calculating the proportion of ways to get 2 girls**:\n   The fraction of the total combinations that contains 2 girls is given as:\n\n   [\n   text{{GirlsProbability}} = frac{text{{NumMembers(bangles)}} choose text{{NumMembers(girls)}}}{text{{NumMembers(total)}} choose text{{NumMembers()}}}\n   ]\n\n   We will also use the previously computed results to plug in:\n\n   [\n   text{{GirlsProbability}} = frac{ frac{text{{NumMembers(girls)}}!}{(text{{NumMembers(girls)}}! times (text{{NumMembers(bangles)}} - text{{NumMembers(girls)}})!)} } { frac{text{{NumMembers(total)}}!}{(text{{NumMembers(girls)}}! times text{{NumMembers(boys)}}!)} }\n   ]\n\n### Rewritten Code\n\n```python\ndef calculate_probability(boys_selected, girls_selected):\n    \"\"\"\n    This function calculates the probability of selecting a certain number of boys and girls from a group, \n    where the total number of boys and girls are given.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    boys_selected (int): The number of boys selected.\n    girls_selected (int): The number of girls selected.\n    \n    Returns:\n    float: The probability of selecting the boys and girls from the group.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    # The total number of ways to select a certain number of boys and girls from a group is given by:\n    total_boys_girls = sum(list(product({0, 1}, repeat=boys_total + girls_total)))  # Girls selected are also counted as boys in this total\n    \n    # The total number of ways to select a certain number of boys from a group and a certain number of girls from the same group is given by product1 and product2 respectively\n    \n    product1 = sum(list(product(range(1, boys_total + 1), repeat=boys_selected))) # Boys selected are also counted as girls in this product\n    product2 = sum(list(product(girls_total, repeat=girls_selected)))\n    \n    # The result is the ratio of product2 to product1\n    \n    return product2 / product1\n```\n\nPlease determine whether the given text is related to computer science, if yes please return \"YES\", else return \"NO\".",
